.TH TUXDIRSTAT 1 "2026-11-06" "TuxDirStat 1.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tuxdirstat, tuxdirstat-gtk3, tuxdirstat-gtk4, tuxdirstat-tk, tuxdirstat-tui \- disk usage analyzer with tree, extension list and treemap
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tuxdirstat
.RI [ folder | snapshot.json | host:/folder ]
.br
.B tuxdirstat
.I output-options
.RI [ other-options ]
.I folder
.br
.B tuxdirstat
.I output-options
.B \-\-import
.I snapshot.json
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B TuxDirStat
scans a folder and shows how its space is used: a tree sorted by size, a list of file
extensions and a shaded treemap where every file is a box whose area matches its size.
.B tuxdirstat
is the Qt interface;
.BR tuxdirstat-gtk3 ,
.B tuxdirstat-gtk4
and
.B tuxdirstat-tk
are the same program with other toolkits, and
.B tuxdirstat-tui
runs in a terminal (ncurses; press \fB?\fR for its keys). All of them share the command line
options below.
.PP
Without an output option a window opens. Giving a folder scans it right away; giving a
snapshot file opens it instead. Without either, the last scan is shown again (this can be
turned off in the View menu).
.PP
A location written as \fIhost\fB:\fI/folder\fR (or \fBssh://\fIhost/folder\fR) is scanned on
another computer over
.BR ssh (1).
This needs key login without a password prompt. The remote side runs TuxDirStat 1.5 or newer
when it is installed and GNU
.BR find (1)
otherwise. Files of a remote scan cannot be deleted from here.
.PP
The window also offers duplicate file search, cleanup suggestions, search, snapshot
comparison, usage by owner, installed packages, btrfs sharing and compression, size history,
HTML/PNG/CSV export, a sunburst view, age coloring, an administrator (read-only) scan
through
.BR pkexec (1)
and, on Arch Linux, package ownership checks.
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
These print results (or write files) and exit without opening a window. They can be
combined; each runs in the order given.
.TP
.B \-\-report
Text report: the largest folders, files and extensions.
.TP
.BI \-\-find " query"
Search the scan. Words match file names (\fB*\fR and \fB?\fR are wildcards). Filters:
\fB>100M\fR, \fB<1G\fR (size), \fBolder:1y\fR, \fBnewer:2w\fR (last change; units h, d, w, m, y),
\fBtype:f\fR or \fBtype:d\fR, \fBext:mp4,mkv\fR, \fBuser:\fIname\fR and \fBpath:\fItext\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-duplicates
List files with identical contents. Sizes are compared first, then the first 64 KB and then
the whole file. Hard links to the same data are not reported.
.TP
.B \-\-cleanup
Caches, build output (node_modules, CMake and Cargo build folders, Python caches), trash,
unfinished downloads, the pacman cache, the systemd journal, crash dumps and unused
Docker, Podman or Flatpak data.
.TP
.B \-\-owners
Disk usage per file owner.
.TP
.B \-\-unowned
Arch Linux: files and folders under /usr, /etc and /opt that no pacman package owns.
Scan / (or one of those folders) for this.
.TP
.BI \-\-export " file"
Save the scan as a snapshot in the ncdu JSON export format.
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " file"
Write every file and folder to a CSV file.
.TP
.BI \-\-compare " snapshot"
Show what grew, shrank, appeared or disappeared since an earlier snapshot.
.TP
.BI \-\-html " file"
Self-contained HTML report with treemap and sunburst images, the largest folders and files
and the file types.
.TP
.BI \-\-png " file"
Treemap image (see \fB\-\-size\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-sunburst " file"
Sunburst image: the folder in the middle, each ring one level deeper.
.TP
.B \-\-btrfs
How much data is shared with snapshots or reflinked copies (deleting it frees nothing) and
how much is stored compressed, per top-level folder and per subvolume. Run as root with
.BR compsize (8)
installed to also see the compressed size on disk.
.TP
.B \-\-history
Size of each top-level item at every recorded date, with a trend line.
.TP
.B \-\-packages
Installed pacman packages and Flatpak apps by size, with orphaned packages marked. Needs no
folder.
.TP
.B \-\-history\-record
Scan every folder listed in \fI~/.config/tuxdirstat/history.conf\fR and save its sizes. The
weekly systemd user timer (Tools \(-> Record size history weekly) runs this.
.SH OTHER OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-import " file"
Read a snapshot (written by TuxDirStat or by \fBncdu \-o\fR) instead of scanning.
Files ending in .gz, .zst, .xz or .bz2 are decompressed first. \fB\-\-export\fR and
\fB\-\-csv\fR compress names ending in .gz and write to standard output for \fB\-\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " n"
Rows per list in \fB\-\-report\fR (default 15).
.TP
.BI \-\-size " width" x height
Image size for \fB\-\-png\fR and \fB\-\-sunburst\fR (default 1600x1000).
.TP
.B \-\-apparent
Use the apparent file size instead of the space used on disk.
.TP
.B \-\-group
Group many similar items (such as game assets) in \fB\-\-report\fR and list the most crowded groups.
.TP
.BI \-\-group\-min " n"
Minimum number of items per group (default 16).
.TP
.B \-\-all\-fs
Cross into other file systems. By default the scan stays on the starting file system
(btrfs subvolumes of the same device are included) and always skips virtual file systems.
.TP
.BI \-\-lang " code"
Interface language (\fBen\fR, \fBtr\fR, ...). The default follows the saved setting, then the
locale. Translations live in \fIpo/\fR in the source tree.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdirstat/settings.conf
Settings shared by all interfaces: size mode, grouping, theme, language, age coloring, sunburst.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdirstat/history.conf
Folders whose size is recorded weekly.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tuxdirstat/history/
Recorded sizes (gzip-compressed snapshots, up to 60 per folder).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/tuxdirstat/last-scan.json.gz
The last scan, shown again at the next start.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tuxdirstat ~/Games
tuxdirstat \-\-report \-\-top 20 /var
tuxdirstat \-\-find '*.iso >1G older:1y' ~
tuxdirstat \-\-export home\-$(date +%F).json ~
tuxdirstat \-\-compare home\-2026\-10\-01.json ~
sudo tuxdirstat \-\-unowned /
tuxdirstat \-\-html report.html \-\-sunburst rings.png ~/Projects
tuxdirstat \-\-report pi:/home
tuxdirstat \-\-packages
tuxdirstat\-tui /var
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if a scan, file or tool failed, 2 for wrong usage.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR du (1),
.BR ncdu (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR compsize (8),
.BR pacman (8)
